# Project rules for AI agents — points-counter

## Code style

- **TypeScript:** stay strict; avoid `any` unless there is a clear, narrow reason.
- **React:** functional components and hooks; keep state and effects easy to follow.
- Names and functions: descriptive, small, single-purpose.

## Naming

- Prefer **kebab-case** for file and directory paths (e.g. `use-round-scores.ts`, `player-row.tsx`).
- Exported symbols stay idiomatic (e.g. `useRoundScores`); **paths** stay kebab-case.
- Avoid cryptic locals (`v`, `d`, `c`, `e`); prefer names like `event`, `nextValue`, `player`, `round`.

## Stack and tooling

- **Stack:** **React 19**, **TypeScript**, **Vite** (e.g. `@vitejs/plugin-react`), ESLint flat config where used.
- **State / persistence:** **MobX** and/or **`localStorage`** for local-first data; no backend unless the product direction explicitly changes.
- After substantive changes, run **`npm run lint`** and **`npm run build`** when those scripts exist; fix anything introduced by the edit.
- Do not add dependencies without a short justification; keep the repo lean unless product needs require otherwise.

## Product context — points-counter

- **Purpose:** count and process **points** for **board / card games** (sessions, players, rounds, history).
- **Local first:** no server; MobX stores persist to **`localStorage`**.
- **Storage keys:**
  - `points-counter-data` — games + `activeGameId` (`schemaVersion` 1)
  - `points-counter-color-mode` — `light` \| `dark`
  - `points-counter-locale` — `en` \| `ru`
  - `points-counter-install-dismissed` — `1` when user dismissed the PWA install prompt
- **Rules (current):** at least 1 player per game (no max); players can join an active game; one open round; close round stores one score per player; negatives allowed; at most one `active` game (switch via Games / Resume).
- **PWA:** installable offline app shell via `vite-plugin-pwa` (manifest + service worker), install prompt UI, offline banner; self-hosted fonts (`@fontsource`). Do not remove icons, manifest fields, or SW registration without replacing them.
- Prefer **simple, accessible UI** (keyboard, labels, readable contrast).

## Editing habits

- Prefer changing existing files over adding new ones when it keeps the design clear.
- Match indentation and formatting already in the file.
- Run the linter after changes; fix any new issues you introduce.

## Component and module layout

- **kebab-case** for component directories (e.g. `score-table/`).
- Per-component layout (when used): `index.tsx`, optional `types.ts`, optional `styles.css` co-located.
- Domain state in hooks or MobX stores (e.g. `hooks/use-session.ts`, `stores/game-store.ts`); components receive data and mutation handlers via props or observers. Ephemeral UI state (sort, search query) may live in leaf components.

## Project structure (evolve as the repo grows)

- Typical layout to aim for:

```
src/
├── components/   # feature UI (kebab-case folders)
├── hooks/        # domain hooks (kebab-case files), if used
├── stores/       # MobX stores when used
├── types/        # shared domain types
├── lib/          # storage helpers, pure utilities
├── assets/       # images, icons
└── main entry + app shell (e.g. main.tsx, App.tsx)
```

- **Changelog:** [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/) at repo root (`CHANGELOG.md`); update for user-visible fixes and behavior changes.
- **README** should stay aligned with real behavior (persistence keys, main flows, scripts, version if shown).
- **Multiple README locales:** if more than one root readme exists (e.g. `README.md` + `README-RU.md`), treat them as one documentation set. When substantive facts change, **update every locale**; wording may differ, technical details must not drift. Cross-link at the top of each file.
- **Vite `define` / build-time version strings:** only add when the UI or logic needs them (e.g. `__APP_VERSION__` with a matching `vite-env.d.ts` entry).

## Commit messages

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/):

`type[optional scope]: description`

- **feat:** new feature (MINOR)
- **fix:** bug fix (PATCH)
- **docs:** documentation only
- **style:** formatting, no code change
- **refactor:** code change that neither fixes nor adds a feature
- **perf:** performance improvement
- **test:** adding or updating tests
- **chore:** build, CI, tooling
- **BREAKING CHANGE:** use `!` after type/scope or footer (MAJOR)

Example: `feat(scoring): add per-round subtotal column`

## Versioning and changelog

### Versioning & Changelog

- Use [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/): MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
- Version lives in root **`package.json`**
- When releasing: bump version, then add a `CHANGELOG.md` entry
- Changelog entries must describe **actual user-facing changes** included in the release (derived from commits/diff since the previous release), not just “bump version”

### Changelog format

- Follow [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/)
- File: `CHANGELOG.md` at project root
- Sections: `### Added`, `### Changed`, `### Deprecated`, `### Removed`, `### Fixed`, `### Security`
- Entry format: `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD`
- Add new entries at the top (above previous releases)
- Do **not** add filler entries like “version bump for release”; if a release contains no notable changes, do not cut a release (or explicitly document why)

## Communication

- Be direct and concise.
- If requirements are ambiguous, ask before large refactors.
- Briefly note non-obvious choices in responses or commits.
